  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "listen",
      "numeral": "I",
      "title": "Listen First",
      "intro": "EQ turns parts of a sound up or down by pitch, from deep lows to airy highs. Before touching anything, look and listen: where does this track's energy sit?",
      "doThis": "Play the busiest section for about ten seconds and watch the spectrum. The shaded areas are where mud and harshness usually build up on a {instrumentName}.",
      "listenFor": "Boomy, cardboard-like tone (mud) or a hard, tiring edge (harshness). Most tracks need a little of one, not both."
    },
    {
      "id": "lowEnd",
      "numeral": "II",
      "title": "Clean the Low End",
      "subtitle": "Make room underneath",
      "intro": "Most sounds carry rumble below their lowest useful note: handling noise, room boom, mic stand thumps. A low cut removes it so the bass and kick have room.",
      "doThis": "Start at {lowCutHz.start} Hz. Slowly raise the low cut until the sound starts to thin out, then back off a little.",
      "listenFor": "The track getting cleaner and tighter, not thinner. If the body disappears, you've gone too far."
    },
    {
      "id": "mud",
      "numeral": "III",
      "title": "Cut the Mud",
      "subtitle": "Find it, then dip it",
      "intro": "Mud is a build-up in the low mids that makes a track sound boomy or boxy. The trick is to find it first, then cut just a few dB.",
      "doThis": "Hold Hear the band and sweep the frequency through the shaded area until it sounds worst. Let go, then turn the gain down a few dB.",
      "listenFor": "The track opening up and getting clearer, while keeping its warmth.",
      "tryThis": "Bypass the band for a moment. If the track sounds better with it off, your cut is in the wrong place."
    },
    {
      "id": "harsh",
      "numeral": "IV",
      "title": "Tame the Harshness",
      "subtitle": "Smooth the edge",
      "intro": "Harshness is a hard, tiring edge in the upper mids. It usually lives in a narrow spot, so a small, narrow cut does the job.",
      "doThis": "Hold Hear the band and sweep through the shaded area to find the sharpest, most painful spot. Let go, keep the width narrow, and cut gently.",
      "listenFor": "The edge softening while the words or notes stay clear. Cut too much and the track goes dull."
    },
    {
      "id": "shape",
      "numeral": "V",
      "title": "Shape the Tone",
      "subtitle": "Small moves, wide curves",
      "intro": "Now the problems are fixed, gently push the tone toward your goal. Good boosts are small and wide: a few dB with a broad curve.",
      "doThis": "Set the band inside the shaded area. Bring the gain up slowly until you hear “{goalName}”, then back off a touch.",
      "listenFor": "{goal.listenFor}"
    },
    {
      "id": "level",
      "numeral": "VI",
      "title": "Level & Compare",
      "intro": "EQ changes how loud a track is, and louder always sounds better. Match the level before you decide whether your EQ helped.",
      "doThis": "Turn Output until the after version matches the original. Then hold Compare to flip between them at the same volume.",
      "listenFor": "Whether the after version is clearer and sits better, not just louder or brighter."
    }
  ],
